msgid ".. that the arrest of Elliot Sinclair can be attributed to Agent 5, Gage Blackwood, who single-handedly defeated Sinclair's ..."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Gage's Apartment (2318 AD)
#. Track ID: INTRO_1
#. Speaker: Gage
msgid "Quick, there's not much time to explain. They'll be here any second. Yes, I am you. I've come from the future. I, you, we've been framed! Now, whatever you try to do, do not interfere with what's about to happen. The suit I'm going to transfer over to you will keep you cloaked, so just stand still and watch. When it's all over, you'll be pulled ten years forward in the future to my present. From there on, it's all up to you. Oh, and I've hidden more information inside of you, you'll figure out what I mean. Here comes the jumpsuit."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Gage's Apartment (2318 AD)
#. Track ID: INTRO_2
#. Speaker: Agent 8
msgid "Come on, Gage, let's go."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Tutorial (No location)
#. Track ID: IO_AUD_1
#. Speaker: Overview
msgid "The jumpsuits navigation buttons allow you to turn left and right, look up and down, and move forward. in the direction you're currently facing. A lit navigation button means that you can turn or move in that direction. You'll stop walking forward when you reach the next decision point."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Tutorial (No location)
#. Track ID: IO_AUD_2
#. Speaker: Overview
msgid "You can scroll through the items in your possession. by clicking the up and down arrow buttons located next to the item list. Clicking the inspect button between the arrows brings up an information screen on the selected item. To pick something up, click and drag it from the view window down into your inventory and let go. To use an inventory item, click and drag its picture icon into the main view window and drop it on top of the object you want to use it on. Items used by or on your jumpsuit are activated by simply clicking on their pictures."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Tutorial (No location)
#. Track ID: IO_AUD_3
#. Speaker: Overview
msgid "Biochips are programmed modules that allow the jumpsuit to perform specific tasks. Biochips may be added to the jumpsuit to give it new functions. To activate a biochip, click on its picture in the inventory item icon window. This will bring up a control panel for that biochip. To get information about what a biochip does, or how it works, click the inspect button."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Tutorial (No location)
#. Track ID: IO_AUD_4
#. Speaker: Overview
msgid "The final element of the jumpsuit's interface is the message window. This display will alert you to any situations or dangerous conditions that you need to be aware of. Pay close attention to the information displayed here. It could save your life."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Tutorial (No location)
#. Track ID: IO_AUD_5
#. Speaker: Overview
msgid "For further information on the jumpsuit and its interface, see the Buried in Time instruction manual. or access the files biochip after starting the game."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "There's definitely something going on on the other side of this door, so obviously there's a way through. Start looking around for a lever or something."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "You may be able to get the spears stopped and the door open, but not for long. I wouldn't try going through unless you could find some way to jam the serpent's jaws open."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H03
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This may sound a little gruesome, but the skull from one of those skeletons we saw would fit pretty nicely in there. And it might just be solid enough to stop those jaws from closing."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H04
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "I don't know what you're thinking, but unless you jam those spears, game over, man!"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H05
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "I remember seeing two skulls. This one here and the one over by where we came in."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H06
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That's more like it, the spears aren't going anymore. I'll bet you could get through the door without jamming the other serpent's head. Of course, you may not be able to get back. Maybe you should also jam the door open, just in case."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H07
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This place gives me the creeps. Quick, try the other serpent heads. Let's find a way out of here."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H08
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Uh, I hate to be the bearer of bad news, but there's no thinking our way out of this one. We'll have to time-jump out and start this time zone over again. This is definitely a two-skull puzzle at least."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H09
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Okay, we have a choice. We can go back the way we came, or we can see what's at the end of the hallway. I vote to go on. There may be another way to open the door down there."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H10
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Well, you're still not going to be able to make it through those spears unless you jam them. But we're out of skulls, unless of course you plan on using your own."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H11
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Do you really have to have the first door jammed? What if you were to use that skull to jam the second set of spears? Then we could at least see what's at the end of this hallway."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H12
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "If you want to get to the other end of this hallway, you're going to need to jam the second set of spears instead of the entrance door."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H13
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Now would be the time to find something to jam the last door open."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H14
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Great. Now we're getting somewhere. So with this third skull, we can go back and jam the last door."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H15
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Well, you don't need the second set of spears jammed to get back to the entrance. So, take out the skull that's jamming them and use it to open the entrance door. Then we'll still have one skull left to jam the second door open."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H16
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Jamming the second set of spears isn't going to help you much if the door isn't open. Why don't you take that skull out and use it to jam the second door?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H17
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Look, you need to get the second door open, right? We've only got three skulls to do it with, so you're going to have to do some juggling. So far, we've tried everything, and the only way we know to open the second door. is the serpent head at the entrance that also opens the first door. So we have to get that one jammed. Now to get to it, we have to leave the first door and the first set of spears jammed. So that leaves only one possible skull that we can take with us. The one that's now jamming the second set of spears! Once we get the second door open, we'll have to figure out a way to get those spears jammed again, but we can cross that bridge when you come to it."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H18
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Okay, we're almost there. If we use the last skull to jam the second door, then all we have to do is find a way to stop the second set of spears and we're through."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H19
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Well, if you remember, Gage, when we open the first door from the entrance, it also opened the second door. So that's the one we need to jam with the last skull."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H20
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Great. The first door is already jammed, so we don't need to be jamming it from the middle of the hallway anymore. That gives us another skull to jam the last set of spears."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H21
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That piece in the middle of the altar looks like it could be taken out. Well, you didn't come here just to look at pretty pictures."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H22
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This altar also has a piece in the middle. I wonder if they worked together in some way."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H23
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Each of the three pieces in these altars have been different sizes. If you put them together, they form a pyramid."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H24
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That piece in the middle of the altar looks like it would fit into the cutout next to the Death God door. Let's try to take it out."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H25
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Another pyramid piece. This must be how you prove that you've been to the other levels."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_H26
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Each piece has had three levels to it. So now we've completed the nine level pyramid and should be granted access to the Realm of the Death God."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_I01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Those spears look easily sharp enough to tear through your suit, but I wouldn't worry about it. Everybody, limbo!"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_I02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Tezcatlipoca was one of the most feared gods. His name means smoking mirror. They called him that because in addition to being a fierce warrior, he was also known for his pranks and illusions. Kind of the Mayan Doug Henning."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_I21
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This chamber seems to be set up as a prayer room. There's an image of the god directly opposite his altar, but no signs of blood suggests that the altar is used in sacrifices, which means that it's probably used for more benign offerings. If I had a nose, I believe I would smell incense."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_I22
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "The trials must be to test the worthiness of the supplicants to the priesthood. Once they make it past a trial, they're allowed in the presence of that god, so that they can pay homage. And then, once they've passed all of the trials and honored each of the gods, they're probably made a priest."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAG_I23
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "You know, it's interesting, all of these gods are Toltec. It looks like they had more influence on Mayan religion here than anyone realized. That really lends a lot of credence to the theory that they were invaders, not just settlers."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAR_H01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That piece in the middle of the altar looks like it could be taken out. Well, you didn't come here just to look at pretty pictures."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAR_H02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This altar also has a piece in the middle. I wonder if they work together in some way."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAR_H03
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Each of the three pieces in these altars have been different sizes. If you put them together, they form a pyramid."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAR_H04
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That piece in the middle of the altar looks like it would fit into the cutout next to the Death God door. Let's try to take it out."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAR_H05
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Another pyramid piece. This must be how you prove that you've been to the other levels."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAR_H06
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Each piece has had three levels to it. So now we've completed the nine-level pyramid and should be granted access to the Realm of the Death God."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAR_I01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This chamber seems to be set up as a prayer room. There's an image of the god directly opposite his altar, but no signs of blood suggests that the altar is used in sacrifices, which means that it's probably used for more benign offerings. If I had a nose, I believe I would smell incense."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAR_I02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "The trials must be to test the worthiness of the supplicants to the priesthood. Once they make it past the trial, they're allowed in the presence of that god, so that they can pay homage. And then, once they've passed all of the trials and honored each of the gods, they're probably made a priest."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYAR_I03
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "You know, it's interesting. All of these gods are Toltec. It looks like they had more influence on Mayan religion here than anyone realized. That really lends a lot of credence to the theory that they were invaders, not just settlers."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYDG_C01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Hey, that's an Environ cartridge, isn't it? And it's loose! It hasn't been incorporated into the artifact like in the other time zones. You should take it. Maybe it'll provide us with some information. Oh, sorry. This one's on the house."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYDG_H01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Judging from the bloodstains, this is either an operating table or a sacrificial altar. Either way, there's been a lot of open heart surgery performed here. I hope that's not what they expect us to do."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYDG_H02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Well, it seems pretty obvious that the Mayan priests required a human sacrifice as the last right of initiation. It's like they put the hearts into that blood-filled basin on the left."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYDG_H03
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Seeing as how they weren't expecting us, they haven't left a victim for the occasion. And frankly, I don't think you would have passed the test. Still, we're going to have to fake it somehow. I don't suppose you've noticed any spare hearts lying around along the way."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYDG_H04
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Careful with this... In my time, the only people who ever tried opening it were found dead. Poisoned! I don't think it'll be very forgiving if you enter the wrong combination of glyphs. You might want to know what it is you're spelling out."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYDG_H06
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "It's been said that the key phrase to open this container. would have been written somewhere on the walls of the temple. But since in our time the pyramid is in ruins, no one has been able to open it."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYDG_H07
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Once again, it comes down to a choice. Try to guess the combination out of a possible, oh, I don't know, 4,096. Or go back to the temple and try to find a message that might hold the key."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYDG_H08
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "What was that inscription in the temple about a holy vessel? If this is the holy vessel that they were referring to, then that message must have been the key."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYDG_I01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "I would imagine that when a supplicant reached this room, there would be a sacrifice victim tied to the altar, and the priest would all be arranged in a circle around the perimeter of the chamber. The only exit from these caverns seems to be through that passage on the other side. So, if you passed the trial, they would pivot the bridge around to let you across. If not, well, I guess you're on your own, buddy. That explains some of the skeletons we found ... trying to find their way out."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYDG_I02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "My, my, my. Snakes, snakes, and more snakes. These guys were really obsessed. Hate to be the guy who had to carve all those. He must have been sick of 'em by the end."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYDG_I03
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "The top of this bridge seems to be a sharpened obsidian blade. Talk about walking gingerly. If one leg slipped off, you'd be beside yourself. I said, \"besides yourself.\" Forget it."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYDG_I04
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That's the Death God, Mictlan."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_H01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Maybe those markings on the door are clues of some sort."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_H02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "We should translate the inscriptions in the lintel above the door and in the headdress on the figure on the right. Any information we could find would really be helpful, Gage."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_H03
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "The inscriptions will help you more than I can. I really think you should translate them."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_H04
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "It looks like we're supposed to put the offering in that mouth to the right of the door. Just keep in mind what they want and what you might need."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_H05
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "So what do you give to the god who has everything? Hey, maybe it's not what you're giving him, so much as what you're giving up."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_H06
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Followers of the Wealth God would probably honor him by giving him something valuable."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_H07
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Something tells me that the Water God wouldn't know what to do with a pair of galoshes or an umbrella. Maybe it's a lot simpler than that."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_H08
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Maybe he would accept rain itself. Or even just water."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_H09
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "How would you prove your worth to the God of War? It's not like we could just go out and join a battle to get through this door."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_H10
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Something related to war. Battle souvenir, maybe? What about something from the siege of Chateau Gaillard?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_H11
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Complete the pyramid. In Mayan mythology, you had to go through each of the other levels of the underworld. to reach the realm of the Death God. Maybe we need something to prove that we've passed the other levels."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_H12
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "If the other doors lead to the other realms of the underworld, then there must be something there that we're supposed to bring back to complete the pyramid."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_I01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "I knew that archaeologists found a series of natural caverns underneath this pyramid, but I don't think anyone's ever been able to figure out what they were used for. The Maya believe that the underworld was entered through a cave. So it's a good bet that that's what this place represented to them. Judging from what we've seen, I'd say that this is some sort of proving grounds, probably for supplicants to the priesthood. And considering that their word for the underworld was Xibalba, which literally means \"Place of Fright,\" I bet this was no funhouse."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_I02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "As I recall, the Mayan heavens and the underworld were usually represented. as a thirteen-step pyramid on top of an upside-down nine-step pyramid, with the Earth as a flat plane in between. The thirteen gods of the heavens ruled the daytime, and the nine gods of the underworld, the night. That's why they called them the \"Lords of the Night.\" Their heavens were pretty exclusive. Only certain people like sacrifice victims and children were let in. The underworld was for everyone else. Anyone that didn't get into heaven would have to make the journey through the nine levels of the underworld. to the dead land, which was ruled over by the God of Death."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_I03
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Well, we're not getting out the way we came in. The opening of that shaft we fell down is at least four meters above us. I guess that's the idea. No other way but forward. I just hope there's another way out of here."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_I04
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That skeleton isn't exactly fresh. They've obviously been using these caverns for a long time. Either that, or they left it here for foreshadowing."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_I05
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Hey, nice door! Didn't I see Bob Vila make one of these on \"This Old Pyramid\"?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_I06
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Gage, relax. It's a door."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_I07
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Wow, the first \"Bazooka Joe\" comic, and they still weren't funny."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_I08
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This is the door to Mictlan, the dead land. The Maya called it that because it was said to be ruled over by Mictlantecuhtli, their god of death."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYMC_I09
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Hmm, you put your offering in this receptacle. The door closes and it's taken by someone or something to be judged for its worthiness. I don't believe in magic or the powers of the gods, but it may be possible that there are acolytes living in alcoves within these walls. And all they do is collect the offerings and open the doors if they're good enough."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYOT_O01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Gage, hello!? *tap* *tap* *tap*. Is this thing on?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYOT_O02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Gage, I'd like to take this time out right now to introduce you to the concept of humor."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYOT_O03
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Gage, are you getting any of this?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYOT_O04
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "K. Read my lips. \"Joke.\""
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYOT_O05
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Gage, are you with me, amigo?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYOT_O06
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Gage, I know you're in there. I can hear you breathing."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_H01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "In our time, this part of the temple is destroyed, so whatever we're looking for must be somewhere else. The question is, how do we get there?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_H02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Well, I would have brushed up on my Mayan if I'd known we were coming here, but I think you're going to have to find some other way to translate this."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_H03
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This may be a stupid question, but why aren't you using your translator biochip?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_H04
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Hey, you're from the future! Doesn't your suit even have a simple translator built in? A calculator? An answering machine? A clapper? Where's Ron Popeil when you need him?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_H05
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Well, unless you're exceptionally gifted, staring at it isn't going to do any good. Our best bet is to go back to your future home, maybe we can find something there."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_H06
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Uh, I wouldn't suggest walking down there. The Maya weren't known for their hospitality."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_H07
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Hmm, the Chac Mool grants entrance to the underworld only on sacred days. Well, the statue is a Chac Mool, but how do we know if today is a sacred day? What happens to us if it isn't?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_H08
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Sacred days. Maybe setting the calendar will trigger something."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_H09
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Well, setting the calendar obviously wasn't enough to get us in. Maybe there's a cover charge."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_H10
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "You know, Gage, I've never seen a Chac Mool turn down a present"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_H11
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "There's got to be something around here that'll fit just right into the Chac Mool's hands. Something round and bowl-like."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_H12
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "You know, I bet this is a schedule of ceremonial days."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_H13
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "I wonder what would happen if we set the calendar to one of these dates?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "I would guess that we're standing on the High Priest's grave pyramid. Historians have theorized that before it was a tomb, it was used for some other ceremonial purpose."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That circular building is the Caracol, which means snail. The Maya built it as an astronomical observatory. They'd line up the sun and moon through rectangular slits in its walls. to identify important days like the summer and winter solstices."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I03
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Behind the observatory is the Casa de las Monjas, or nunnery."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I04
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That huge pyramid is the Castillo. It's dedicated to Kukulcan, who is the Mayan equivalent of the Aztec serpent god, Quetzalcoatl. The structure behind it is the Temple of the Warriors."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I05
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "An interesting thing about El Castillo is that it was built on top of a smaller, older pyramid. In fact, some of the other Mayan pyramids have been built over the previous pyramid as many as six times, with the old temples still remaining underneath."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I06
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Ah, the Pelota field. The Maya played a game which was kind of a cross between basketball and soccer. Without using their hands or feet, they'd have to knock a hard rubber ball through a slightly larger stone hoop as high as 30 feet off the ground. No such thing as sore losers, mainly because the losers were decapitated."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I07
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Overlooking the ball court is the Temple of the Jaguars, which also function as box seats for the royalty."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I08
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That square building over there is the Casa Colorada. Historians believe it was the house of the high priests."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I09
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Those are Mayan hieroglyphs. They had a pretty sophisticated pictograph writing system. Unfortunately, my Mayan's a little rusty right now."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I10
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "I think this relief is depicting a lower priest, or supplicant to the priesthood, making an offering to the water god. Now, if I'm not mistaken, that symbol represents a cave inside a mountain. The Toltecs believe that the underworld was entered through a cave."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I11
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This is a little hard to see, but I think it's depiction of a ceremony. It looks like a priest or supplicant is making an offering to a god."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I12
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Gage. Gage, look at this! This is a representation of one of the nine trials of the underworld. That's definitely Tezcatlipoca, the Toltec god of war. The legend says that when a person died, their soul would have to travel through eight levels of the underworld on route to the Deadland. On each one they'd have to pass a trial given by the god that ruled that level. After passing all of the trials, they'd be allowed into the Deadland where they spend eternity without joy or suffering."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I13
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That's a Chac Mool. They were pretty common in Chichen Itza. Generally, if you wanted to be accepted by the gods, you'd place an offering in the chacmool hands. I've only seen pictures of them facing away from the temple, though. I wonder why this one's facing inward."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I14
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This is a Mayan ceremonial calendar. The larger disc has twenty month names inscribed in it, and the smaller one numbers the days. When they're spun, they make a month-day combination where they meet. If you do the math, that's 260 different combinations, each of which represent a day of the ceremonial year."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I15
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Historians think that certain days of the ceremonial year. were reserved for honoring the gods with human sacrifices, bloodletting, and other fun, rainy day activities. Hey, what can you say? I guess the Mayans can be summed up with one simple word: Fun."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I16
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This is a list of some sort. I'm not sure what it represents, but each column seems to end with a date."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I17
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This looks like an ordainment ceremony. That guy with his head lowered is probably a supplicant to the priesthood who's just passed his right of initiation."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I18
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "It seems like these scenes are showing ceremonies that take place in this temple. It was destroyed long before anyone had a chance to record any of these murals. So, were the very first ones to see what it might have been used for."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I19
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Considering that the priests are wearing their best jaguar hides, I'd say this scene is depicting a pretty important religious ceremony."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYTP_I20
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "The blue stuff in the bowl is called pom. It's a kind of incense. The Maya used it as an offering to the gods."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_C01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Ooh, rope bridge, very dangerous. You go first."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Ah, afraid of heights, huh? Well, just think of it as summer camp. There's even a river down there ... uh ... somewhere."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Oh, come on. Don't wimp out on me now. We have to get across and as much as you look like the FTD florist in that suit, I don't think you're about to sprout wings and fly across."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H03
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "The inscription above the door said that the followers of the God of Wealth. must turn their backs to worldly possessions and lower their heads in humility. It sounds like it's meant figuratively, but could it also be literal?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H04
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "If the inscription is taken literally, \"wealth\" may be referring to the gold room. If we stand with our back to the room and look down at the opposite wall of the chasm, what will we see?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H05
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Yeah, right. Take a look around you. If there was a way out of here, don't you think that Twiggy and Skeletor here would have found it by now? No. Look, this is definitely a trap. Looks like our only choice is to time jump out and try again."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H06
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This must be the punishment for dishonoring the God of wealth. Remember? \"Turn your back to worldly possessions and lower your head in humility?\" Next time, try turning around and looking down after crossing the bridge. instead of being blinded by all the shiny, sparkly things."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H07
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Looks like we're going to have to find some way to climb down to that lower passage."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H08
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Well, some rope would help, or maybe we could tie a few bed sheets together."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H11
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That piece in the middle of the altar looks like it could be taken out. Well, you didn't come here just to look at pretty pictures."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H12
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This altar also has a piece in the middle. I wonder if they work together in some way."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H13
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Each of the three pieces in these altars have been different sizes. If you put them together, they form a pyramid."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H14
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That piece in the middle of the altar looks like it would fit into the cutout next to the Death God door. Let's try to take it out."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H15
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Another pyramid piece. This must be how you prove that you've been to the other levels."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H16
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Each piece has had three levels to it. So now we've completed the nine level pyramid and should be granted access to the Realm of the Death God."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H21
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That piece in the middle of the altar looks like it could be taken out. Well, you didn't come here just to look at pretty pictures."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H22
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This altar also has a piece in the middle. I wonder if they work together in some way."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H23
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Each of the three pieces in these altars have been different sizes. If you put them together, they form a pyramid."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H24
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That piece in the middle of the altar looks like it would fit into the cutout next to the Death God door. Let's try to take it out."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H25
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Another pyramid piece. This must be how you prove that you've been to the other levels."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_H26
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Each piece has had three levels to it. So now we've completed the nine-level pyramid and should be granted access to the realm of the Death God."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_I01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Isn't rope bridge an oxymoron?"
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_I02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Man, this place looks like something Wayne Newton would wear around his neck."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_I03
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That's the wealth god, Tepiyollotl."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_I04
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Hey, wow! Nice work. Do you have any idea how difficult it is to carve stone? And all these guys had to work with were primitive stone tools and a spirograph."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_I11
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This chamber seems to be set up as a prayer room. There's an image of the god directly opposite his altar, but no signs of blood suggest that the altar is used in sacrifices, which means that it's probably used for more benign offerings. If I had a nose, I believe I would smell incense."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_I12
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "The trials must be to test the worthiness of the supplicants to the priesthood. Once they make it past the trial, they're allowed in the presence of that God, so that they can pay homage. And then, once they've passed all of the trials and honored each of the gods, they're probably made a priest."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_I13
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "You know, it's interesting, all of these gods are Toltec. It looks like they had more influence on Mayan religion here than anyone realized. That really lends a lot of credence to the theory that they were invaders, not just settlers."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_I21
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This chamber seems to be set up as a prayer room. There's an image of the god directly opposite his altar, but no signs of blood to suggest that the altar is used in sacrifices, which means that it's probably used for more benign offerings. If I had a nose, I believe I would smell incense."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_I22
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "The trials must be to test the worthiness of the supplicants to the priesthood. Once they make it past the trial, they're allowed in the presence of that god, so that they can pay homage. And then, once they've passed all of the trials and honored each of the gods, they're probably made a priest."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWG_I23
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "You know, it's interesting. All of these gods are Toltec. It looks like they had more influence on Mayan religion here than anyone realized. That really lends a lot of credence to the theory that they were invaders, not just settlers."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_C01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Hey, who's the architect who built this bridge? This place is like the Winchester Mystery Pyramid."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_C02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Ah, very clever, grasshopper."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_H01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Well, they look a little rickety, but they'll probably hold your weight. Maybe we should cross and see if there's any way to get up to that door."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_H02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Careful not to jump too late. Try to lead the platform so it'll catch you on the down swing."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_H21
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "that piece in the middle of the altar looks like it could be taken out. Well, you didn't come here just to look at pretty pictures."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_H22
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This altar also has a piece in the middle. I wonder if they work together in some way."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_H23
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Each of the three pieces in these altars have been different sizes. If you put them together, they form a pyramid."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_H24
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That piece in the middle of the altar looks like it would fit into the cutout next to the Death God door. Let's try to take it out."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_H25
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Another pyramid piece. This must be how you prove that you've been to the other levels."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_H26
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Each piece has had three levels to it. So now we've completed the nine level pyramid and should be granted access to the realm of the Death God."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_I01
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This is a pretty elaborate contraption. The Maya were amazingly advanced, but I never would have imagined this level of mechanical engineering. From the sound of it, there's a water wheel down below that's driving this whole mechanism. That tells me that there must have been a particular season for these priesthood initiation trials. Part of the reason the rain god was so important to the Maya was that the Yucatan was susceptible to drought. If we'd arrived a few months later, there would have been little more than a trickle to drive these platforms."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_I02
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Mmm, he's pretty. That would be Tlaloc, the Rain God."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_I03
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "That's the god Quetzalcoatl, or Kukulcan, as the Mayans called him. He had at least three different aspects: a feathered serpent, an old man, and the charming gargoyle you see in front of us."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_I04
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "Kukulkan was also the name of the Toltec chieftain who led his people to the Yucatan Peninsula. and settled them in Chichen Itza. And that's why his mug is plastered *everywhere*."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_I21
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "This chamber seems to be set up as a prayer room. There's an image of the god directly opposite his altar, but no signs of blood to suggest that the altar is used in sacrifices, which means that it's probably used for more benign offerings. If I had a nose, I believe I would smell incense."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_I22
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "The trials must be to test the worthiness of the supplicants to the priesthood. Once they make it past the trial, they're allowed in the presence of that god, so that they can pay homage. And then, once they've passed all of the trials and honored each of the gods, they're probably made a priest."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Chichen Itza, Mexico (1050 AD)
#. Track ID: MYWT_I23
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "You know, it's interesting, all of these gods are Toltec. It looks like they had more influence on Mayan religion here than anyone realized. That really lends a lot of credence to the theory that they were invaders, not just settlers."
msgstr ""

#. Location: Unknown Environment
#. Track ID: _CGTT_I0
#. Speaker: Arthur
msgid "The wooden roofs were covered with tin to prevent being set ablaze by nasty flaming, uh ... ... *ahem* ... uh, nasty flaming arrows."
msgstr ""

